<%- include('partials/head') %>

<div class="hero">
  <h1>Stops raids with something you can take back.</h1>
  <p>
    A raid bot wrongly banned the person who built this, because it could not tell a crowd
    arriving from a crowd attacking. Everything here is built to avoid making that mistake
    again.
  </p>
  <p class="stat beta">
    <span class="live" aria-hidden="true"></span>
    Closed beta, open to <strong>Protection Suite</strong> servers
  </p>
  <div class="cta">
    <a class="btn primary" href="/pricing">Join the beta</a>
    <a class="btn" href="/raidprotector/privacy">What it stores</a>
  </div>
  <p class="hint">
    Report mode is the default. A new install watches and acts on nobody until you say otherwise.
  </p>
</div>

<section class="block">
  <div class="block-head"><h2>What happens during a raid</h2></div>
  <ol class="steps">
    <li>
      <div>
        <strong>Signals fire, measured against your server</strong>
        <span>A join burst above what is normal here. Ten joins a minute is an emergency in one
          server and a Tuesday in another, so the threshold is learned rather than set.</span>
      </div>
    </li>
    <li>
      <div>
        <strong>Two independent signals before anyone is touched</strong>
        <span>Joining during a burst is one. Posting the same text as fifteen others is another.
          One alone is not enough, because being present is not taking part.</span>
      </div>
    </li>
    <li>
      <div>
        <strong>Timeouts, not bans</strong>
        <span>A timeout stops a raid just as dead and expires by itself. Bans are off by default,
          and when they are on they wait behind a delay anyone can cancel.</span>
      </div>
    </li>
    <li>
      <div>
        <strong>One button undoes all of it</strong>
        <span>Every action is recorded with what was there beforehand, so the whole incident
          reverses in one press. If part of it cannot be undone, it names that part.</span>
      </div>
    </li>
  </ol>
</section>

<section class="block">
  <div class="block-head"><h2>How it fails</h2></div>
  <p class="lede">
    The failure it is built to avoid is banning a real member. Every path resolves toward doing
    less.
  </p>
  <dl class="modes">
    <div class="mode safe">
      <span class="dot"></span>
      <div>
        <dt>It is not sure</dt>
        <dd>It does nothing irreversible. Unclear is a real answer, and the timeout that already
          stopped the raid expires on its own.</dd>
      </div>
    </div>
    <div class="mode safe">
      <span class="dot"></span>
      <div>
        <dt>A long-standing member is caught up in it</dt>
        <dd>Anyone who has been here for weeks is reported to you, never auto-punished. Raiders
          can fake an account's age. They cannot fake a month in your server.</dd>
      </div>
    </div>
    <div class="mode safe">
      <span class="dot"></span>
      <div>
        <dt>Your server gets popular overnight</dt>
        <dd>Two hundred people arriving from a video look identical to a raid in the numbers. It
          reads what they wrote, so a rush of "hi" does not come out the same as two hundred
          identical slurs.</dd>
      </div>
    </div>
    <div class="mode alert">
      <span class="dot"></span>
      <div>
        <dt>It fires repeatedly in a short time</dt>
        <dd>A circuit breaker stands it down. Several separate incidents in ten minutes means the
          configuration is wrong, not that the server is under sustained attack.</dd>
      </div>
    </div>
    <div class="mode alert">
      <span class="dot"></span>
      <div>
        <dt>It has just been added</dt>
        <dd>It will not act until it can see your member list, because until then a long-standing
          member looks like a stranger.</dd>
      </div>
    </div>
    <div class="mode crit">
      <span class="dot"></span>
      <div>
        <dt>Its own code has been changed</dt>
        <dd>It checks itself against a record of what was deployed. If anything differs it stops
          acting on people everywhere at once and keeps reporting, rather than enforcing rules
          nobody wrote.</dd>
      </div>
    </div>
  </dl>
</section>

<section class="block">
  <div class="block-head"><h2>What it cannot do</h2></div>
  <dl class="modes">
    <div class="mode alert">
      <span class="dot"></span>
      <div>
        <dt>Work without reading messages</dt>
        <dd>Telling a video shout-out apart from a raid cannot be done by counting, so unlike
          BotApprove this one does hold the Message Content intent. Text is reduced to a
          fingerprint in memory and never written to disk. The
          <a href="/raidprotector/privacy">privacy policy</a> says exactly what is kept.</dd>
      </div>
    </div>
    <div class="mode alert">
      <span class="dot"></span>
      <div>
        <dt>Catch a patient attacker</dt>
        <dd>Detection is thresholds and fingerprints. Someone who stays under yours and varies
          their wording will not trip it.</dd>
      </div>
    </div>
    <div class="mode crit">
      <span class="dot"></span>
      <div>
        <dt>Act on the server owner</dt>
        <dd>Never, under any evidence. A bot that can depose an owner is a worse weapon than the
          one it defends against.</dd>
      </div>
    </div>
  </dl>
</section>

